Recently, projects that were compliable using `^9.4.0` broke because of a breaking change that escaped into a patch. I imagine this was a simple mistake, so I'm just letting you know.

The commit [bc3a283](https://github.com/sindresorhus/ora/commit/bc3a28363a9873304ffb56420cb55fe83a7f41e8#diff-7aa4473ede4abd9ec099e87fec67fd57afafaf39e05d493ab4533acc38547eb8R166) changed the interface in a breaking matter by widening the function, and already implemented functions were not ready to handle the broader inputs.
```diff
- failText?: string | ((error: Error) => string) | undefined;
+ failText?: string | ((error: unknown) => string) | undefined;
```

Versions: 9.2.0 and 9.3.0 (latest)

9.1.0 and earlier works as expected

Basically, running this snippet:

```ts
import ora from "ora";

const spinner = ora("Spinning…").start();

await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 5000));

spinner.stop();
```

Doing Ctrl+C while the spinner is on doesn't kill the process - The process has to be killed through `kill -9` or `killall node`

Node version: 24.11.0
Also happens with Bun version: 1.3.1

// node version 16
const checking = ora();
checking.succeed('success'); // will exit the process Immediately

// node version 19 or 21
const checking = ora();
checking.succeed('success'); // does not exit the process until timeout

My code is something similar to this and I use ora to wait for a promise to end.
```js
import ora from 'ora';
import readline from 'readline';

const rl = readline.createInterface({
    input: process.stdin,
    output: process.stdout
});

async function longProcess() {
    const spinner = ora("Loading").start();
    return new Promise((resolve) => {
        setTimeout(() => {
            resolve("Hello");
            spinner.stop();
        }, 1000);
    });
}

function getInput() {
    rl.question("> ", async (input) => {
        let response = await longProcess();
        console.log(response);
        getInput();
    });
}

getInput();
```
However the application just abruptly closes after `.stop()` is called and doesn't allow me to input again.

